#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/id #uuid "7ad46566-b122-5de3-9b87-9beec9ac6761" :question/math-academy-id "e-21877" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.25\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t) = 3\\sin(6t)\\,\\textrm{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nmy'' = -ky + F(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.25y'' =-ky + 3\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 4ky = 12\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos \\omega t + B\\sin \\omega t\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega$ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4k \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac14\\omega^2\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force\n\n$$\nf(t)=12\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nwhich is $6$, is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac14\\omega^2 = \\dfrac14\\cdot6^2 = 9 \\,\\textrm{kg/s}^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231270 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "f41473ff-05a6-5f3e-ae5b-0584810aa0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21878" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t)$.\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n3. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$, and $\\phi$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega$.\n\nThe external force is\n\n$$\nf(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nwhich is sinusoidal with frequency $4$. Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4$.\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n2. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n3. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t))\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = f(t)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[8(D - 2Ct)\\cos (4t) - 8(C + 2Dt)\\sin (4t)] + 16t(C\\cos (4t) + D\\sin (4t)) &= 4\\cos (4t) + 12\\sin (4t) \\\\\n8D\\cos (4t) - 8C\\sin (4t) &= 4\\cos (4t) + 12\\sin (4t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients,\n\n$$\nC=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n3. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231380 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/id #uuid "bb405211-5b18-5cc1-a264-bdf6e2fbcc60" :question/math-academy-id "e-6735" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 25y =2\\sin^2{pt}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 25\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 5\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{5 t} + B\\sin{5 t}\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $2 \\sin^2 pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega = 5$. Since\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2 \\sin^2 pt = 1 - \\cos 2pt\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p$. Therefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when\n\n$$\n2p = 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "26f1f833-70f9-57ff-a5a2-3b166b487bf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230249}] :question/id #uuid "8f6ac78e-cb74-5768-92ef-290d5885de7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343459" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4kg$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t) = 2\\cos (5t)N$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}} $kg/s^{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369135} {:answer/id #uuid "1632a717-fa9b-5a41-b58e-6cbba7f3e9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369136} {:answer/id #uuid "5be7ba38-0784-532d-823b-79721c845b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369137} {:answer/id #uuid "30dcb20d-e5bc-50aa-a16d-c0cb4a88e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369138} {:answer/id #uuid "dca16548-7f00-5f60-a5f4-041400b2a11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369135} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7490e5a-e807-53a6-b133-4feed29aa08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512495a2-8bfb-52b3-bf2e-01e8a787e6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343761" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=-2.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91716b9b-25b8-5f7d-af82-c5c6244ffece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231216} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d47177-e7eb-5bbe-ad61-37d6ebe28dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231217} {:answer/id #uuid "f093da8e-4b94-5386-8861-cef36519b4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231218} {:answer/id #uuid "2456a120-3251-59f3-b369-f5d67663ae79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231219} {:answer/id #uuid "532c87ef-52c1-5250-9bf9-3d5e673bd018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2456a120-3251-59f3-b369-f5d67663ae79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231219} :answer-field/id #uuid "1232421e-2c06-5494-9d63-831097f76c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231215}] :question/id #uuid "a6af51c6-8a3b-5e30-952e-168721f14820" :question/math-academy-id "q-64650" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 2\\cos^{2} pt$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?"}]}
